cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "zone-server-h2o: A zone server for the V-Sekai multiplayer fabric"
abstract: >
  Host for the minimum user-generated-content game loop. Keeps zone
  entity state in FoundationDB as integer micrometers, and runs
  sandboxed guest programs. It has no networking of its own. Script guests run in-process under libriscv against an
  eight-call domain ABI. Engine guests run as separate processes under
  Bubblewrap. ReBAC gates both planes.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-07"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/zone-server-h2o"
url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/zone-server-h2o"
keywords:
  - multiplayer
  - foundationdb
  - riscv
  - sandbox
  - rebac
  - godot

references:
  - type: software
    title: "libriscv: A Fast and Compact RISC-V Sandbox for Games and Embedded Use"
    authors:
      - family-names: Walla
        given-names: "Alf-André"
    url: "https://github.com/libriscv/libriscv"
    notes: >-
      Vendored at thirdparty/libriscv. Runs script guests in-process
      against src/sandbox/zone_abi.h. Chosen for the rollback path
      because serialize_to and forking give bit-exact snapshot and
      replay, which multiplayer-fabric-manuals rfd/0098 measures.

  - type: software
    title: "H2O: An Optimized HTTP/1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3 Server"
    authors:
      - family-names: Oku
        given-names: Kazuho
    url: "https://github.com/h2o/h2o"
    notes: >-
      Used only as an event loop and cross-thread message queue. It
      serves no HTTP path in this repository.

  - type: software
    title: FoundationDB
    authors:
      - name: "Apple Inc."
    url: "https://github.com/apple/foundationdb"
    notes: >-
      Linearizable datastore for zone entity state and for the guest
      key-value store in src/sandbox/zf_guest_kv.c.

  - type: software
    title: "QCBOR: A Comprehensive, Powerful, Commercial-Quality CBOR Encoder/Decoder"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lundblade
        given-names: Laurence
    url: "https://github.com/laurencelundblade/QCBOR"
    notes: >-
      Vendored at thirdparty/QCBOR for the guest ABI wire format.

  - type: software
    title: "Bubblewrap: Unprivileged Sandboxing Tool"
    authors:
      - name: "containers project"
    url: "https://github.com/containers/bubblewrap"
    notes: >-
      Runs engine guests as separate processes. --unshare-net is what
      enforces the rule that guests never reach the networking loop.

  - type: software
    title: CrucibleBench_Phase1
    authors:
      - name: CrucibleBench
    url: "https://github.com/CrucibleBench/CrucibleBench_Phase1"
    license: MIT
    notes: >-
      Source of the Middleham MUD scenario that was ported to
      mud/guest/mud_guest.cpp: Source_Code/state_machine.py's
      MiddlehamStateMachine (rooms, NPCs, items, commands, trust and
      suspicion, objectives) and Source_Code/classifier.py's
      _fallback_classification(). The port deviated in one documented
      way, using a ChaCha20 PRNG in place of CPython's Mersenne
      Twister. Commit f96d5b2da removed the MUD subsystem, so that file
      is no longer in the tree. This entry stays. Credit for work that
      shaped a project does not expire when the derived file is
      deleted, and a reader of the history needs the provenance.

  - type: generic
    title: "multiplayer-fabric-manuals: Architecture Decision Records for the V-Sekai multiplayer fabric"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/multiplayer-fabric-manuals"
    notes: >-
      Decisions of record for this repository, including rfd/0092 on
      ReBAC planes, rfd/0094 and rfd/0095 on the guest loop and the two
      guest classes, and rfd/0096 through rfd/0100 on measured
      transport, storage, rollback and bandwidth budgets.
